  {
    "album": "Julius Eastman Vol. 5: Gay Guerrilla",
    "artist": "Wild Up",
    "score": "",
    "url": "https://avantmusicnews.com/2026/05/11/amn-reviews-amn-reviews-wild-up-julius-eastman-vol-5-gay-guerrilla-2026-new-amsterdam-records/",
    "source": "Avant Music News",
    "pub_date": "2026-05-11",
    "tags": ["experimental", "weird", "progressive", "avant-garde"],
    "excerpt": "Gay Guerrilla is the fifth release in Wild Up's Eastman series. Each of these offerings is excellent, and this year's take is one of the most assured installments. Wild Up extends the original four-piano score into chamber orchestra form, with strings, horns, reeds, and (of course) pianos. Played expertly, Gay Guerrilla is exhilarating and hypnotic. The album takes the classical tradition's decorum and mutilates it into something furious and alive. Bravo.",
    "site_id": "avantmusicnews",
    "crawl_status": "success"
  },
  {
    "album": "Alive Alone, Alive Together",
    "artist": "Ava Mendoza",
    "score": "",
    "url": "https://avantmusicnews.com/2026/05/10/amn-reviews-ava-mendoza-alive-alone-alive-together-2026-burning-ambulance-music/",
    "source": "Avant Music News",
    "pub_date": "2026-05-10",
    "tags": ["experimental", "weird", "progressive", "avant-garde"],
    "excerpt": "Alive Alone, Alive Together collects Mendoza's recent live recordings from four different events. Half are duets with drummer Hamid Drake from the Summer Bummer Festival in Antwerp, while the rest are solo performances from the US and Italy. Despite what might be thought of as a limited sound palette, there is little repetition throughout the album. These are matter-of-fact guitar heroics without any sense of ego. Or restraint.",
    "site_id": "avantmusicnews",
    "crawl_status": "success"
